---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const themes = [
  'Financial independence and the responsibility to understand one\'s own financial life',
  'Marriage and the stories couples agree to believe',
  'The difference between deception and choosing not to look',
  'Family reputation and inherited narratives',
  'Motherhood and what parents pass to their children',
  'Institutional silence and self-protection',
];
---

<BaseLayout
  active="novel"
  title="The Family Narrative — A Novel by Monica Messer"
  description="The Family Narrative is Monica Messer's debut novel — a story of marriage, money, reputation, and the stories families preserve until someone decides the truth matters more."
>
  <!-- BOOK INTRO -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container split">
      <div class="split-media">
        <img src="/assets/book-cover.png" alt="The Family Narrative by Monica Messer" class="cover-img" />
      </div>
      <div class="split-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">A Novel</p>
        <h1 style="font-size:clamp(2rem,4.4vw,3.2rem);">The Family Narrative</h1>
        <p class="lede">Every family has a story.</p>
        <p>
          Frances Stanton has spent decades building a life that works. A respected professor. A beautiful
          home in Los Gatos. Two sons nearly grown. A husband whose family name opens doors at the
          university, in the community, and in every room that matters.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then, over lunch on her fifty-fourth birthday, Frances overhears a conversation that makes
          her question everything she thought she knew about her marriage.
        </p>
        <p>
          At first, she believes the betrayal is an affair. But as the truth surfaces, Frances discovers
          something far larger: hidden debt, addiction, family complicity, institutional silence, and a
          carefully protected version of the Stanton story that everyone around her seemed to understand
          before she did.
        </p>
        <p>
          When her husband files for divorce before she has even caught up to the truth, Frances is forced
          to confront not only what he has hidden, but what she allowed herself not to examine—especially
          the financial life she trusted him to manage.
        </p>
        <p>
          To move forward, she must learn how to protect herself without losing her compassion, how to
          tell her sons the truth without asking them to inherit its burden, and how to decide what parts
          of the Stanton story are worth carrying forward.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>The Family Narrative</em> is a novel about marriage, money, reputation, and the stories
          families preserve—until someone decides the truth matters more.
        </p>
        <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1.2em;">
          What do we owe the stories we inherit—and what do we choose to pass on?
        </p>
        <div class="btn-group">
          <a href="/read/" class="btn btn-primary">Read Chapter One</a>
          <a href="/read/#listen" class="btn btn-outline">Listen to Chapter One</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- THEMES -->
  <section class="bg-sand">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">At the heart of the novel</p>
      <ul class="theme-list">
        {themes.map((theme) => (
          <li>{theme}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- BOOK CLUB -->
  <section>
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why Book Clubs Will Want to Read The Family Narrative</p>
      <p>
        <em>The Family Narrative</em> is the kind of novel that can keep a book club talking long after
        everyone has finished the last page.
      </p>
      <p>
        At its center is a marriage coming apart, but the questions the novel raises are much larger than
        whether Frances should stay or leave. How much of our past do we owe a partner before asking them
        to build a life with us? When does trusting a spouse become surrendering responsibility for our
        own financial life? And can one person deliberately control information while the other still bear
        some responsibility for not asking enough questions?
      </p>
      <p>
        The novel also gives readers something book clubs thrive on: characters whose choices can be
        understood without necessarily being defended. Dick is shaped by addiction, privilege, and a family
        that repeatedly protects him from consequences, raising the question of whether compassion and
        accountability can exist at the same time. His parents force another difficult conversation: When
        does mercy become enabling, and can the very kind of second chance that saved one generation become
        destructive when repeated in the next?
      </p>
      <p>
        Frances's choices create equally rich debate. As she tries to protect her teenage sons, readers must
        consider how much truth children need about the adults they love—and whether shielding children from
        painful realities can sometimes perpetuate the very secrecy a parent hopes to end.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ultimately, <em>The Family Narrative</em> asks a question nearly every reader can bring back to their
        own life: What do we want to inherit from our families, and what are we willing to leave behind? The
        novel does not offer easy villains, easy absolution, or easy answers. Instead, it gives readers
        competing loyalties and morally complicated choices—and then lets the book club decide where it stands.
      </p>
      <p><em>That is where the conversation begins.</em></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
